{"html":"<h1 id=\"vkf-016-strict-vector-lifting-and-axis-reductions\">VKF 0.1.6 — Strict Vector Lifting And Axis Reductions</h1>\n<p>VKF 0.1.6 replaces compatibility-filtered aggregate broadcasting with a smaller, stricter rule: ordinary typed functions may lift recursively through vectors, while tuples and records remain explicit whole values.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-changed-from-015\">What changed from 0.1.5</h2>\n<ul><li>Automatic lifting descends only through fixed or dynamic vector layers.</li><li>A lifted element must match the function parameter type exactly. Ordinary scalar conversions still apply to direct calls, but do not select vector leaves.</li><li>Tuples and records are atomic. Their arithmetic requires an explicit operator overload instead of silently transforming selected fields.</li><li>Exact whole-container matches still take priority. A row function can therefore map over a matrix without a user-written loop.</li><li>Unary and binary overload families are resolved from typed aliases before machine lowering. The backend no longer guesses that an arbitrary aggregate should use an unrelated overload.</li><li>Higher-order callables and generic multiset containers retain safe, arity-aware overload fallback when their frontend representation is erased.</li><li><code>stat.sum</code> reduces all nested vector dimensions when <code>axis</code> is omitted.</li><li><code>stat.sum(axis:)</code> accepts one constant integer or a nonempty tuple of constant integers for fixed rectangular numeric vectors. Negative axes count from the last dimension; duplicates and out-of-range axes are compile errors.</li><li>Sum results retain the numeric vector leaf type when it is statically known.</li><li>Landing-page benchmark tables now present measurements and same-host ratios without exposing internal acceptance thresholds or pass labels.</li><li>Relative performance targets remain available as explicit development checks; release evidence collection does not turn runner-specific ratios into a correctness verdict.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"verification\">Verification</h2>\n<ul><li>320 native VKF tests pass with zero failures.</li><li>The suite includes explicit rejection coverage for tuple/record lifting, conversion-selected vector leaves, implicit record arithmetic, duplicate and out-of-range axes, and tuple arguments to <code>stat.sum</code>.</li><li>Positive coverage includes fixed and dynamic vector lifting, nested vectors, vector-of-record calls, row functions over matrices, recursive full sums, integer/negative/tuple axes, alias-aware overloads, integer variadic sums, resource ownership, and multiset arithmetic.</li><li>All 59 documented programs are rebuilt and executed as part of release proof.</li><li>Windows x64, Linux x64, and macOS ARM64 proof reports bind outputs and timing samples to exact source and compiler hashes.</li><li>The latest <a href=\"../../../reference/benchmarks/core-comparison/results/linux-x64-016.html\">same-host Linux comparison</a> and <a href=\"../../../reference/benchmarks/policy-landscape/evidence/windows-x64-v0.1.6-ci.html\">256-policy Windows landscape</a> are retained with the release evidence, including every timing sample and code hash.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"native-scope\">Native scope</h2>\n<p>The included native modules remain <code>math</code>, <code>stat</code>, <code>random</code>, <code>time</code>, <code>io</code>, <code>collections</code>, <code>errors</code>, <code>system</code>, <code>process</code>, and <code>regex</code>. <code>physics</code>, <code>ui</code>, and <code>symbolic</code> remain excluded rather than shipping partial implementations.</p>","headings":[{"level":1,"title":"VKF 0.1.6 — Strict Vector Lifting And Axis Reductions","id":"vkf-016-strict-vector-lifting-and-axis-reductions"},{"level":2,"title":"What changed from 0.1.5","id":"what-changed-from-015"},{"level":2,"title":"Verification","id":"verification"},{"level":2,"title":"Native scope","id":"native-scope"}],"examples":[],"so